**Why it works:** every subarray ends somewhere, so the maximum over `end[i]` covers all of them. Extending a negative-sum prefix can only hurt, which is exactly what the `max(x, ...)` encodes: restart when the running sum would drag `x` down.\n\n**Alternative views the interviewer may raise:** prefix sums (answer = max over i of prefix[i] - min prefix before i), and divide-and-conquer in O(n log n) which returns the best crossing the midpoint; the latter is the only reason this problem is sometimes filed under divide and conquer.\n\n**Common mistakes:** initialising `best` to 0 (fails when all elements are negative), allowing an empty subarray, and resetting `cur` to 0 instead of to `x`.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return the subarray indices? (Record a tentative start whenever `cur` restarts and commit it when `best` improves.) Circular array? (max(Kadane, total - min subarray), with the all-negative special case.) Maximum sum with length at most k? (Prefix sums plus a monotonic deque.) Maximum product instead of sum? (Track min and max, see Maximum Product Subarray.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["maximum-product-subarray","maximum-sum-circular-subarray","best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock"],"number":129,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def maxSubArray(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}